## Purpose

Most things worth building get decided in two steps that people tend to blur together: **evaluating** an idea and **committing** to it.

Evaluation is often a live event — a pitch to investors, a design review, a proposal presented to the people who'd have to build it. The room's reaction is real signal, and it's gone the moment the room empties. Commitment is different. Choosing where to spend months of your working life deserves the document, not just the talk: six minutes tells you whether someone can present, but the written proposal tells you whether the scope is real, whether the stack is one you'd want to work in, and whether there's something in it *you'd* want to build. Teams that commit in the room, to whoever is standing nearest, often commit to the wrong thing.

Today is the first step. Every student delivers **Pitch #1** — defending their idea and recruiting others to it — while everyone else evaluates each pitch against the same shared criteria. The ideas that recruit become the **anchor projects** that teams form around. *(In this course, you commit on {{ S11_DAY }}, after a weekend with the proposals — see below.)*

Both jobs are graded acts of professional judgment. How you present is the pitch; how fairly you score someone else's is the same skill you'll use in design review, code review, and every hiring or funding decision you ever help make.

---

## Learning objectives

By the end of this session, you'll be able to:

1. **Deliver a persuasive technical pitch** under time pressure, and handle live questions honestly.
2. **Evaluate peers' pitches consistently** against shared criteria — each criterion on its own, the evidence rather than the confidence.
3. **Record a considered preference** — rank the projects you'd join with reasons tied to the criteria, not to who your friends are, and revise it once you've read the documents.
4. **Separate evaluating from committing** — explain why the two deserve different amounts of time, and defend a choice made with the documents in hand.

---

## What we'll do together

**A few minutes of calibration first.** The run of show, the ground rules (the clock is real; score during the Q&A; keep your scores to yourself; questions aimed at the idea, never the person), a quick look at the seven criteria you've used since Week 3, and one more pass at the recruiting question. Everyone in the room has their own project, so *"no, I have my own idea"* tells a presenter nothing. The honest answer is what you'd say if you walked in with no project and were looking for something worth a semester — and a **why** about the *project* ("I don't believe the scope") is far more useful than a why about *you*.

**Then the pitches — most of the session.** Each one is **6–8 minutes plus 2–3 minutes of Q&A**, on a visible timer. You score **every pitch except your own** on a paper half-sheet, and you **finish that sheet before the next pitch begins** — scores written at the end of the block are scores of a memory.

**Then selection, in the room.** Three inputs get blended: the **peer scores**, the **recruiting interest** (the "would you join?" counts and their whys), and the **instructor's judgment** against the same criteria, with final say. Out of that come the **anchor ideas** — projects that can carry a team — plus any approved **solo paths**. An idea that isn't selected **didn't lose**: its founder chooses an anchor to join, bringing everything their discovery taught them, or continues solo at solo scope. Solo is a real outcome, not a consolation prize.

**Then preferences — quietly, and privately.** On the **Team Preference Worksheet** you rank your **first, second, and third choice** among the anchors, each with a line of why in criteria language; say whether you'd **rather continue solo** and at what scope; and note what you'd bring to a team. Ranked choices let nearly everyone get their first or second pick, where a show of hands only records where people happened to be standing. *"My friend is on it" is not a reason.* **Nobody sees anyone else's.**

